# Project Architecture

## Overview

This is a **fantasy football** application built with **Next.js (App Router)** deployed on **Vercel**. The backend is a separate AWS codebase providing APIs. This repo owns the frontend UI, the design system, and a thin BFF (Backend for Frontend) layer via Next.js Route Handlers.

**This is a single deployable frontend package** with no cross-repo dependencies. Backend API contract types are maintained locally in `src/types/api-contract.ts`, synced manually with the backend repo when contracts change. The design system is colocated as a directory (`src/design-system/`), not a separate package.

## Server Components vs. Client Components

Next.js App Router uses **Server Components by default** (SSR). This affects how the codebase is structured:

- **Server Components** (default — no directive needed):
  - `src/app/*/page.tsx` — page-level components that fetch data and pass it as props
  - `src/app/*/layout.tsx` — layouts that wrap pages
  - Any component that only renders HTML from props with no interactivity

- **Client Components** (require `'use client'` at top of file):
  - Most `src/design-system/` components — anything using `useState`, `useEffect`, `useRef`, event handlers (`onClick`, `onChange`), or browser APIs
  - `src/features/` hooks that use React state or effects

- **The boundary pattern:**
  ```tsx
  // src/app/players/page.tsx — Server Component (fetches data)
  import { PlayerTable } from '@/design-system';

  export default async function PlayersPage() {
    const players = await fetchPlayers();  // Server-side fetch, no loading spinner
    return <PlayerTable players={players} />;  // Pass data as props to client component
  }

  // src/design-system/data-display/PlayerTable.tsx — Client Component (interactive)
  'use client';
  export function PlayerTable({ players }: PlayerTableProps) {
    const [sortBy, setSortBy] = useState('points');  // Client-side interactivity
    // ...
  }
  ```

- **Rule:** Place `'use client'` as high as needed but as low as possible. Don't make a whole page a Client Component when only one child needs interactivity.

## Directory Structure

```
src/
├── app/                      # Next.js App Router — pages, layouts, API routes
│   ├── layout.tsx            # Root layout (providers, fonts, global styles)
│   ├── page.tsx              # Home / dashboard page
│   ├── (auth)/               # Auth route group (login, callback, logout)
│   ├── roster/               # /roster — lineup management
│   ├── matchups/             # /matchups — weekly matchup views
│   ├── players/              # /players — player search, stats, rankings
│   ├── waivers/              # /waivers — waiver wire
│   ├── trades/               # /trades — trade proposals
│   └── api/                  # BFF Route Handlers (see bff.mdc)
│       ├── auth/             # Cognito auth (login, callback, logout, session)
│       ├── players/          # Proxy to AWS player data APIs
│       └── league/           # Proxy to AWS league APIs
├── design-system/            # Reusable UI components and tokens (library code)
│   ├── primitives/           # Button, IconButton, Badge, Avatar, Input, Select, Toggle, Tooltip, Divider, Skeleton
│   ├── player/               # PlayerMiniCard, PlayerCardCompact, PlayerCardDetailed, PlayerStatRow, PlayerCompareTray
│   ├── data-display/         # StatTable, StatBar, StatSparkline, ScoreIndicator, RankBadge, MatchupDifficulty, EmptyState
│   ├── layout/               # Card, Modal, Tabs, Sidebar, TopNav, PageLayout, Section
│   ├── fantasy/              # LineupSlot, RosterGrid, MatchupCard, WaiverCard, WeekSelector (ScoreboardTicker, TradeBlock deferred)
│   ├── tokens/               # Color, typography, spacing token exports
│   └── index.ts              # Public API — barrel export (the ONLY import point)
├── features/                 # App-specific feature logic (not pages, not design system)
│   ├── auth/                 # useSession hook, auth state helpers
│   ├── roster/               # Roster management hooks and logic
│   ├── players/              # Player search, filtering, sorting hooks
│   └── matchups/             # Matchup comparison logic
├── lib/                      # Shared utilities
│   ├── api-client.ts         # Typed fetch wrapper for BFF endpoints
│   └── constants.ts          # App-wide constants
└── types/                    # App types and API contract
    ├── api-contract.ts       # Backend API DTOs (synced from footballBackEnd)
    └── league.ts             # League, Roster, Matchup
```

## Design System First (CRITICAL)

All UI work starts in the design system. This is the workflow for any visual change:

1. **New UI element?** Build the component in `src/design-system/` first. Write tests (TDD). Add a Storybook story. Export from the barrel file. Only THEN consume it from `src/app/`.
2. **Modifying an existing UI element?** Change it in `src/design-system/` first. Update tests. Verify in Storybook. The app pages automatically pick up the change.
3. **Never build a one-off UI component directly in `src/app/`.** If it's visual and reusable, it belongs in the design system. If it's truly page-specific layout glue, it can live in the page file — but the building blocks it uses must come from the design system.

This ensures the design system is always the source of truth for how things look and behave.

## Import Boundary Rules (CRITICAL)

1. **`design-system/` is a self-contained library.** It NEVER imports from `app/`, `features/`, `lib/`, or `types/` outside its own directory.

2. **App code imports ONLY from `design-system/index.ts`** (the barrel file).
   ```typescript
   // ✅ CORRECT
   import { Button, PlayerCardCompact, StatTable } from '@/design-system';

   // ❌ WRONG — reaching into internal paths
   import { Button } from '@/design-system/primitives/Button';
   ```

3. **The design system NEVER imports:**
   - App state, contexts, or providers
   - Next.js routing (`next/navigation`, `next/router`)
   - API clients or data-fetching logic
   - Feature-specific business logic

4. **Design system components receive data via props only.** They are pure, presentational, and app-agnostic.

5. **`features/` contains app logic but not UI.** Features export hooks and utilities. Pages in `app/` compose features with design-system components.

## Codebase Separation

- **This repo:** UI, design system, BFF proxy layer, client-side state/caching.
- **AWS backend repo (separate):** API endpoints, business logic, database, Cognito User Pool config.
- **Local contract copy:** backend API DTOs live in `src/types/api-contract.ts`, synced manually with the backend repo.
- The BFF layer in `src/app/api/` is intentionally **thin** — it proxies, authenticates, and aggregates. Real business logic lives in the AWS backend.
